{"parentUuid":"5cff4b73-56c0-4e0b-bbe3-797fd6c2aaf1","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad3b5c11f3a618bca","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CffZUYvNibvunqVianXne","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both cells are implemented and committed locally, but nothing is pushed, no PR is open, and nothing is merged. `sr-checks` still refuses the work, and the repo's push gate (`verify-before-push`) requires a clean run.\n\n**Cells**\n- **codex × task-stream-frames: supported.**\n  - Run: I recorded `codex-mock/snapshots/runs/task-stream-frames`. Codex announces a sub-agent with the `spawn_agent` `collab_tool_call` item, started then completed with the thread id and state `pending_init`. The status and end appear only in the `wait` item, as state `completed` plus the message.\n  - Deviations: Codex has no task frames, and a shell command is never a task. The mock takes a sub-agent's answer from the script, and `spawn_agent` / `wait_agent` fire no hooks.\n- **cursor × task-stream-frames: supported.**\n  - Run: I recorded `cursor-mock/snapshots/runs/task-stream-frames`, using the 2026.09.28 build. A sub-agent and a background command are announced by their `tool_call` frames (`taskToolCall`, and `shellToolCall` with `isBackground` and `shellId`). The end of a background command is a system `task_notification` frame, after the `awaitToolCall`.\n  - Deviations: Cursor has no `task_started` or `task_updated` frame, and a sub-agent gets no notification. The mock takes the sub-agent's reply from the script and fires no hooks for these calls.\n\n**What I implemented**\n- Both mocks report tasks through the shared `internal/tasks` code, with `sr:provides` markers.\n- New files:\n  - `codex-mock/internal/runner/background.go`, `codex-mock/internal/runner/background_frames.go` and `codex-mock/internal/events/collab.go`\n  - `cursor-mock/internal/runner/background.go` and `cursor-mock/internal/runner/background_frames.go`\n- Each mock has an e2e test marked `sr:proves`: `collab_test.go` for codex and `task_frames_test.go` for cursor.\n- `internal/tasks/module.yaml` now lists the two `background*.go` globs as module home.\n- I made one-line edits to `host.go` and `run.go` in the cursor runner, and to `turn_host.go` and `run.go` in the codex runner. Those files carry other capabilities' markers.\n\n**Local checks:** build, vet, gofmt, the codex tests, the `internal` tests and my two new tests pass. One existing cursor test, `TestATimedOutHookThatFailsClosedBlocksTheCommand`, failed once on a timing assertion while the machine was loaded, and I did not re-run it.\n\n**What `sr-checks` still refuses**\n1. **My own, and I fixed it.** My tests did not check the background command's task id or that the codex command is no task. I added those assertions in the second commit. I have not re-run `sr-checks` since.\n2. **My own, needs your decision.** A judge flagged `concern-undeclared` for the new `reply` key in the script's tool-call input, which supplies a sub-agent's answer in both new adapters. It says nothing decides how a mock fakes a sub-agent, and it wants an ADR. Claude's mock runs a real nested sub-agent instead, so the two disagree. I did not invent an ADR. Do you want an ADR for scripted sub-agent answers, or a different approach?\n3. **Refusals about other capabilities, not caused by my change.** Several `capability-rigor` refusals concern `stop-hook-payload`, `noninteractive-run`, `user-prompt-submit-hook`, `session-end-hook` and `session-start-hook`. They ask for tests that earlier PRs never had. They are re-judged now only because I touched `run.go` and `turn_host.go`.\n\nI stopped here rather than rewrite other capabilities' tests. Once you decide item 2, I can push, open the PR and watch CI. The remaining item 3 refusals would still need your call, since I won't merge with a failing check."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_to